{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310533,"nonce":"z/3ASOPoHNh0/jEqvqDdecB0m4s=","title":"OPEN: every assignment in expression position yields its value by RE-READING the lvalue (tast_to_hir.rs:2404). Dead load after every store; ...","message":"`tast_to_hir.rs:2404`, in the `BinaryOperator::Assign | *Assign` arm:\n\n```rust\n// Create a variable reference to the assigned value\nlet result_expr = self.lower_expression(left);\n```\n\nAn assignment in expression position is lowered to a block\n`{ lhs = rhs; \u003cre-read of lhs\u003e }`. Haxe yields the **assigned value** (for a\nproperty, the **setter's return**), never a fresh read of the target.\n\n**Statement position goes through this path too.** `TypedStatement::Assignment`\n(:1474) does *not* re-read, but a bare `b.name = \"x\";` still reaches :2404 —\nproven by MIR, so the defect is not confined to expression contexts.\n\n\nMIR for `b.name = \"x\";` where `name` is `(get, set)` over `_n`:\n\n```\n$10 = call fn3($9, $8)   ; set_name\n$12 = call fn2($11)      ; get_name   \u003c- the re-read\n```\n\nand inside `set_name`, `_n = v;` emits `store` then a **dead** `load` of the\nsame slot.\n\n1. **Dead load after every store** — pervasive, costs code size everywhere.\n2. **A property's getter is called on every write.** Measured against the\n   oracle: `+2` getter calls per write (one at the write site, one from\n   `return this.name = v` inside the setter), `+1` per read. This is what makes\n   accessor-counting probes disagree with haxe even when the *values* are right.\n3. **It refuses valid Haxe.** `var name(never, set)` that the program only ever\n   writes fails with `[E0100] Cannot read from write-only property (Null or\n   Never getter)` (`field/access.rs:543`) — the compiler errors on a read *it\n   synthesised itself*.\n\n\nThe natural fix is to bind the RHS to a temp and yield the temp:\n\n```rust\nlet (tmp, sym) = self.gen_temp_var();   // :4185\n// Let tmp = rhs;  lhs = tmp;  =\u003e tmp\n```\n\nEvaluation order is fine — MIR's `Assign` already lowers the RHS first\n(`stmt/statements.rs:603`, before the lvalue's own subexpressions), so hoisting\nit into a preceding `Let` preserves the current order exactly.\n\n**Ownership is what breaks.** `stmt/statements.rs:923-940`:\n- `rhs_is_owned_allocation` requires `HirExprKind::New`, so with a temp the RHS\n  becomes `Variable(tmp)` and the *lhs* stops being tracked;\n- ownership is transferred off a `Variable` RHS **only when the lhs is a\n  Field/Index** (`lhs_is_field`).\n\nSo for `x = new Thing()` with a plain-variable lhs, the temp stays tracked and\nis freed at scope exit while `x` still points at it. Every heap assignment in\nthe program reaches this path, so a blanket temp is the silent-corruption\ncategory. A targeted version — temp only when the target is a property with a\nnon-`Default` getter — is safe on ownership grounds (property targets are\nalways Field lvalues, covered by `lhs_is_field`), but `tast_to_hir` has no\nsymbol -\u003e property map today; it only clones `field.property_access` at :636.\n\nBest correct answer is probably to yield the **setter's return value**, which\nMIR already computes and discards at `field/lvalue.rs:208`.\n\nRelated: [[bugs-abstract-properties-need-setter-writeback]],\n[[feature-isvar-backing-storage]].\n\n- Index summary moved from MEMORY.md (2026-10-18): OPEN: every assignment RE-READS its lvalue (tast_to_hir.rs:2404) — dead load after every store, a real extra getter call per property write, and it REFUSES valid Haxe for `(never,set)`. The naive temp fix BREAKS OWNERSHIP.\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `bugs_assignment_rereads_its_target` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}